// ============================================================================
// Resolution · TYPE ARGUMENT BINDING  (generic type parameter -> concrete type)
//
// For a written generic type reference (List<Foo>, Map<String,Long>, extends
// Base<Foo>), bind each of the generic type's declared type PARAMETERS to the
// concrete type ARGUMENT supplied at that reference.
//
// Keyed by the base reference hash — the SAME hash carried by field_type_resolves /
// local_var_type_resolves / method_param|return_type_resolves — so a receiver's
// declaration site joins directly to its bindings. This is per-SITE: List<Foo> and
// List<Bar> are distinct base refs with distinct bindings; there is no global
// "List.E -> X" fact.
//
// Consumed in expression-resolution to substitute a type-variable member type at a
// receiver's site: a method whose return type is the type variable E, called on a
// List<Foo> receiver, returns Foo. The existing per-entity resolutions are left
// untouched — they resolve concrete written types and simply yield nothing for a
// TYPE_VARIABLE member, so this sits ON TOP as an additive union branch.
//
// Only covers WRITTEN type arguments. Inferred forms (diamond `new ArrayList<>()`,
// `var`, method type inference, inferred lambda params) supply no argument to bind
// and require flow / target typing — not handled here.
//
// Type-argument reference structure (java_type_reference): the base type is depth
// "0"; each type argument is a depth-"1" reference whose parentReferenceHash (col 5)
// is the base ref and whose position (col 6) is the argument index. The argument
// refs are client references (the generic use is written in client code); the type
// PARAMETERS come from whichever side declares the generic base type — java_* for a
// client generic, lib_* for a library generic (List/Map/… — the common case).
//
// java_type_reference (18): 5 parentReferenceHash 6 position 7 depth 17 uniqueHash
// java_type_parameter / lib_type_parameter (8): 0 paramName 1 position
//   6 typeRegistryLinkHash(owner) 7 typeParameterUniqueHash
// ============================================================================

// ── type_arg_binding(BaseRefHash, BaseTypeHash, ParamName, ArgTypeHash) ─────

// Client generic base type (a client type declared with <…>).
type_arg_binding(baseRef, baseType, paramName, argType) :- type_ref_resolves(_, baseRef, baseType),
    java_type_parameter(paramName, pos, _, _, _, _, baseType, _),
    java_type_reference(_, _, _, _, _, baseRef, pos, "1", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, argRef),
    type_ref_resolves(_, argRef, argType).

// Library generic base type (List, Map, Optional, … — the dominant case).
type_arg_binding(baseRef, baseType, paramName, argType) :- type_ref_resolves(_, baseRef, baseType),
    lib_type_parameter(paramName, pos, _, _, _, _, baseType, _),
    java_type_reference(_, _, _, _, _, baseRef, pos, "1", _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, argRef),
    type_ref_resolves(_, argRef, argType).

// ── INHERITED generics: climb a binding to the SUPERTYPE that declares the method ─
//
// The two rules above bind only the type parameters of the receiver's OWN declared
// type. But a generic method is usually inherited: `ArrayList<String> l; l.get(0)` —
// get() is declared on List, returning List's type variable E, not ArrayList's. The
// binding must reach List. It does, because ArrayList's `implements List<E>` clause
// records — positionally — that List's parameter at position p is filled by ArrayList's
// type variable at that position. Following those edges up the hierarchy carries the
// concrete argument to whichever ancestor actually declares the called method, renaming
// the type variable at each level (List<E> → Collection<E> → Iterable<T>: E becomes T).
//
// super_tvar_rename(Sub, Super, SuperParamName, SubVarName): in Sub's extends/implements
// clause, Super's type parameter SuperParamName is supplied by Sub's type variable
// SubVarName (a depth-1 TYPE_VARIABLE argument at the parameter's position). The Super
// side is always a LIBRARY type here — the only type-variable methods substituted
// downstream (expr-type.dl) are lib_method_ret_tvar — so Super's parameters come from
// lib_type_parameter; Sub may be a lib type (ArrayList) or a client subtype of one.

// Supertype references carrying their own hash, so the depth-1 type arguments (whose
// parentReferenceHash is that hash) can be read back. lib_* for a lib subtype's clause,
// type_* (client) for a client subtype's clause.
lib_super_ref_h(sub, superName, sref) :- reached_lib(sub),
    lib_type_reference(_, "SUPER_TYPE", sub, _, _, _, _, "0", superName, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, sref).
lib_super_ref_h(sub, superName, sref) :- reached_lib(sub),
    lib_type_reference(_, "IMPLEMENTS_INTERFACE", sub, _, _, _, _, "0", superName, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, sref).
type_super_ref_h(sub, superName, sref) :-
    java_type_reference(_, "SUPER_TYPE", sub, _, _, _, _, "0", superName, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, sref).
type_super_ref_h(sub, superName, sref) :-
    java_type_reference(_, "IMPLEMENTS_INTERFACE", sub, _, _, _, _, "0", superName, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, sref).

// Positional rename, lib subtype → lib supertype. lib_type_parent pins the resolved
// super; matching super's simple name to the clause's typeName ties the correct
// supertype reference (sref) to it. The depth-1 TYPE_VARIABLE argument at Super's
// parameter position names Sub's type variable that fills it.
super_tvar_rename(sub, super, superParam, subVar) :- lib_super_ref_h(sub, superName, sref),
    lib_type_parent(sub, super),
    lib_type(superName, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, super),
    lib_type_parameter(superParam, pos, _, _, _, _, super, _),
    lib_type_reference("TYPE_VARIABLE", _, _, _, _, sref, pos, "1", _, _, subVar, _, _, _, _, _, _, _).
// Positional rename, client subtype → lib supertype (class Foo extends ArrayList<String>).
super_tvar_rename(sub, super, superParam, subVar) :- type_super_ref_h(sub, superName, sref),
    type_parent(sub, super),
    lib_type(superName, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, super),
    lib_type_parameter(superParam, pos, _, _, _, _, super, _),
    java_type_reference("TYPE_VARIABLE", _, _, _, _, sref, pos, "1", _, _, subVar, _, _, _, _, _, _, _).

// Transitive climb: a binding on Sub carries to each Super via the positional rename,
// closing over the whole ancestor chain (bounded — the type hierarchy is a finite DAG).
type_arg_binding(ref, super, superParam, argType) :- type_arg_binding(ref, sub, subVar, argType),
    super_tvar_rename(sub, super, superParam, subVar).

// ── CLIENT method returning its owner class's type variable (U getU() on Holder<U,…>) ──
// The library counterpart is lib_method_ret_tvar (lib-hierarchy.dl); this is the client
// mirror, so a generic client class instantiated with concrete arguments substitutes its
// own methods' type-variable returns the same way. Layout matches lib_method_ret_tvar:
// java_method owner is col 8; the METHOD_RETURN TYPE_VARIABLE reference names the class
// type variable (col 11) and links back to the method (col 16, kind col 17).
java_method_ret_tvar(m, owner, tv) :- java_method(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, owner, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, _, m),
    java_type_reference("TYPE_VARIABLE", "METHOD_RETURN", _, _, _, _, _, "0", _, _, tv, _, _, _, _, m, "METHOD", _),
    tv != "".
